CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=8582954a9e4c6f0259c6236be794d7d3

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SECRETS
# Left blank here; real values live only in .env.local (gitignored).
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Create as an account token (Manage Account -> API Tokens, not My Profile -> API Tokens), named
# "R2 - tools/r2 apply", with the account-scoped Account > Workers R2 Storage > Edit permission --
# like AI Gateway and D1, R2 doesn't expose a narrower per-bucket scope than that. Used by
# apply.ts, which shells out to `wrangler` -- wrangler reads this env var itself, same as
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID above. Unverified whether R2's admin API rejects Account-Owned tokens the
# way tools/workers' Workers Builds API does (see that package's .env) -- try this first; if
# apply.ts gets a 401 regardless of permissions, fall back to a User API Token instead.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
